<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At last, to address the class imbalance inherent in the original dataset, we employed a data augmentation strategy to upsample minority classes to a target size of 5000 samples per class. Specifically, we applied a range of physiologically plausible signal transformations—including temporal shifting, polarity flipping, time warping, baseline drifting and amplitude scaling—to generate synthetic ECG samples. All augmentations were applied channel-wise with controlled intensity. This augmentation was applied exclusively to the training set and resulted in a balanced training set of 30,000 samples, while the validation and test sets remained unchanged. In summary, each sample in our dataset comprises two synchronized signal modalities: the standard 12-lead surface ECG and 6 intracardiac electrograms (IEGM).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Architecture</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a lightweight dual-branch framework composed of two modality-specific ResNet encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, a residual semantic attention fusion module, and a Transformer Encoder-based classification head.
Given the two modalities for each sample: the standard 12-lead surface ECG signal <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{(e)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 12\times 977}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>977</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{(e)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 12\times 977}</annotation></semantics></math> and a 6-channel intracardiac electrogram (IEGM) signal <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 6\times 977}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>977</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 6\times 977}</annotation></semantics></math>, the network outputs unnormalized classification logits <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=6</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of diagnostic classes. During inference, a softmax is applied to the logits to obtain class probabilities.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>ResNet-based Modality Encoders</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each modality is processed independently by a modified 1D ResNet encoder to extract a compact feature vector:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{ResNet}_{e}(\mathbf{x}^{(e)})\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ResNet</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{ResNet}_{e}(\mathbf{x}^{(e)})\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{ResNet}_{m}(\mathbf{x}^{(m)})\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ResNet</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{ResNet}_{m}(\mathbf{x}^{(m)})\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The encoder begins with a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> convolution (stride 2), batch normalization, and a non-inplace ReLU, followed by a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> max pooling (stride 2). Four residual stages progressively increase the channel dimension <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{64,128,256,256\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>64,128,256,256</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{64,128,256,256\}</annotation></semantics></math> while reducing temporal resolution, with two residual blocks per stage. Each block adopts a pre-activation-like pattern: <em id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Conv–BN–ReLU, then Conv–BN, followed by identity addition and a final ReLU</em>, with an optional <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> projection on the skip path when dimensions differ. Finally, adaptive average pooling reduces the temporal dimension to one, producing a fixed 256-D vector per branch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Attention-based Cross-modal Semantic Alignment Fusion</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{q_{\cdot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{q_{\cdot}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{k_{\cdot}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{k_{\cdot}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{v_{\cdot}}\in\mathbb{R}^{256\times 256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{v_{\cdot}}\in\mathbb{R}^{256\times 256}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}^{q_{\cdot}},\mathbf{b}^{k_{\cdot}},\mathbf{b}^{v_{\cdot}}\in\mathbb{R}^{256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo></msub></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>256</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}^{q_{\cdot}},\mathbf{b}^{k_{\cdot}},\mathbf{b}^{v_{\cdot}}\in\mathbb{R}^{256}</annotation></semantics></math> be learnable projection weights and biases for queries, keys, and values, respectively. Given the encoder outputs <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 256}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gated cross-modal features are computed as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma\!\left(\frac{\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{q_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{q_{e}}\big)\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{k_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{k_{m}}\big)}{\sqrt{h}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>h</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma\!\left(\frac{\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{q_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{q_{e}}\big)\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{k_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{k_{m}}\big)}{\sqrt{h}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{v_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{v_{m}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{v_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{v_{m}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma\!\left(\frac{\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{q_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{q_{m}}\big)\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{k_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{k_{e}}\big)}{\sqrt{h}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>h</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma\!\left(\frac{\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}\mathbf{W}^{q_{m}}+\mathbf{b}^{q_{m}}\big)\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{k_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{k_{e}}\big)}{\sqrt{h}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{v_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{v_{e}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\odot\big(\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)}\mathbf{W}^{v_{e}}+\mathbf{b}^{v_{e}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Hadamard (elementwise) product, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h{=}256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h{=}256</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. The gated features are concatenated with the originals and fused by</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{LN}\!\Big(\mathrm{ReLU}\big([\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}]\mathbf{W}^{f}+\mathbf{b}^{f}\big)\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.423em;" width="1.423em"><mi>LN</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathrm{LN}\!\Big(\mathrm{ReLU}\big([\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\bar{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(e)},\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{(m)}]\mathbf{W}^{f}+\mathbf{b}^{f}\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{1024\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{1024\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>512</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}^{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{512}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> is the fused feature vector. We apply dropout with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math> inside the fusion layer before layer normalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>3 </span>Transformer Encoder-based Classification Head</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fused cross-modal feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> is processed by a lightweight Transformer encoder head. We reshape <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> to a length-<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}512</annotation></semantics></math> scalar sequence and linearly project each token with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and bias <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}^{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d{=}64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d{=}64</annotation></semantics></math> is the model dimension. A standard sinusoidal positional encoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is added to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then fed into a single Transformer encoder layer (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> heads, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{ff}}{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{ff}}{=}128</annotation></semantics></math>, dropout <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, pre-norm, GELU activation):</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}=\mathrm{Encoder}(\mathbf{h}_{0};d,h,d_{\mathrm{ff}},p)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Encoder</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ff</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}=\mathrm{Encoder}(\mathbf{h}_{0};d,h,d_{\mathrm{ff}},p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The encoder output <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is average-pooled over the sequence length to obtain <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, then passed through layer normalization (with affine parameters) and a fully connected layer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> with bias <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}^{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}^{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the logits:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\bar{\mathbf{h}}\,\mathbf{W}^{c}+\mathbf{b}^{c},\quad\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\bar{\mathbf{h}}\,\mathbf{W}^{c}+\mathbf{b}^{c},\quad\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times C}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of diagnostic classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Global Class-aware Contrastive Loss</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contrastive learning has been widely applied in biomedical representation learning under supervised<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, self-supervised<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and semi-supervised<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> paradigms. Here, we focus on the supervised paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. However, the traditional supervised contrastive loss relies on pairwise intra-class attraction, but for underrepresented classes, this can lead to unstable optimization and noisy gradients, resulting in scattered or fragmented embeddings. Moreover, the traditional supervised contrastive loss treats all sample pairs equally—uniformly pulling intra-class pairs together and pushing inter-class pairs apart. While effective in balanced settings, the traditional supervised contrastive loss becomes suboptimal under imbalance, where minority classes receive insufficient optimization focus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The instability of pairwise intra-class alignment, especially in few-shot scenarios, has been mitigated by introducing statistical or learnable class prototypes to provide stable semantic anchors and enhance class-wise cohesion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Meanwhile, to address limitations of symmetric contrastive objectives under class imbalance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> introduces an averaging-based correction to balance the influence across classes, but it does not account for sample difficulty. Similarly, RCL also applies class-number-based weighting to mitigate imbalance, but still overlooks the mining of hard samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. In contrast, many hard negative mining methods adopt instance-pair-level weighting based solely on similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, lacking global class-level statistics and exhibiting high sensitivity to batch composition—issues that worsen under few-shot settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the above limitations, we propose a novel loss function called Adaptive Global Class-aware Contrastive Loss (AGCACL). We retain the previous notations, where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the embedding of sample <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> its corresponding label, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> the batch size, the total loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{AGCACL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>AGCACL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{AGCACL}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\alpha_{y_{i}}\left(\ell_{\text{intra}}^{(i)}+\ell_{\text{inter}}^{(i)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>intra</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>inter</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\alpha_{y_{i}}\left(\ell_{\text{intra}}^{(i)}+\ell_{\text{inter}}^{(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{\text{intra}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>intra</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{\text{intra}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\psi_{y_{i}}\cdot\left(1-\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}}{\|\mathbf{z}_{i}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}\|_{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\psi_{y_{i}}\cdot\left(1-\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}}{\|\mathbf{z}_{i}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}\|_{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{\text{inter}}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>inter</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{\text{inter}}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}j=1\\
j\neq i\end{subarray}}^{N}\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}\cdot\exp\left(\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{j}}{\tau\|\mathbf{z}_{i}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{z}_{j}\|_{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}j=1\\
j\neq i\end{subarray}}^{N}\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}\cdot\exp\left(\frac{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{j}}{\tau\|\mathbf{z}_{i}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{z}_{j}\|_{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a class-wise repulsion coefficient that modulates the inter-class similarity contribution, and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of intra-class alignment for each class. These parameters allow the model to adaptively emphasize certain class relationships during optimization. <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learnable prototype of class <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is jointly optimized during training to represent the semantic center of the corresponding class in the embedding space. <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> adjusts the overall loss contribution of class <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to account for data imbalance. <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a class-specific balancing weight designed to mitigate the influence of class imbalance by reweighting each instance’s contribution to the total loss. Finally, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter that scales the inter-class contrastive component <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\text{inter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> to control the softness of similarity scores. In the following, we describe the rationale and computation of each key component in AGCACL. We begin with the intra-class alignment term and class-level weighting, followed by the design of the inter-class contrastive formulation. Finally, we present our adaptive class-aware modulation coefficients, which serve as a core contribution of this framework.
Overall, the AGCACL possesses the following properties:(1) <span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Globally informed</span>: Both <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are derived from the full training set, rather than individual batches, which is especially important in few-shot scenarios where batch composition may be highly variable. (2) <span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantically grounded</span>: The formulation allows the incorporation of semantic priors or domain-specific class relations, enabling optional manual adjustments to inject expert knowledge.
(3) <span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data-driven and adaptive</span>: Coefficients are updated with a momentum-based smoothing strategy, enabling stable adaptation to evolving embeddings while maintaining compatibility with stochastic training and random initialization.
(4) <span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class-aware and robust</span>: Unlike uniform pair-wise weighting, our class-level coefficients <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> adaptively modulate alignment and repulsion per class, improving resilience to imbalance and semantic ambiguity, especially in few-shot settings.
(5) <span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simple and interpretable</span>: Avoids complex optimization by using straightforward similarity measures and structured normalization for inter-class modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In II-C Adaptive Global Class-aware Contrastive Loss ‣ II Methods ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), to improve representation stability in few-shot regimes, we replace pairwise intra-class attraction with a center-based alignment mechanism, where a learnable class prototype <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the semantic anchor for each category. This formulation is inspired by the parametric contrastive framework introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which incorporates class-wise learnable centers into contrastive learning. By treating class prototypes as jointly optimized parameters, this approach enables more consistent gradients and facilitates learning under limited data conditions.
However, unlike intra-class attraction, inter-class separation aims to model local decision boundaries. Therefore, we retain the pairwise formulation to reflect finer class-level distinctions within the batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce the dominance of majority classes, we assign each instance a class-aware weight <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the inverse class frequency over the <em id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entire</em> training set (excluding data augmentations). Let <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vector of class frequencies, where each entry corresponds to the number of samples in a particular class, and let <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a small constant. We compute a temperature-scaled softmax over inverse frequencies:</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{1}{\tau_{\alpha}}\cdot\frac{1}{\mathbf{f}+\epsilon}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mi>softmax</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{1}{\tau_{\alpha}}\cdot\frac{1}{\mathbf{f}+\epsilon}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and use <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to reweight the loss of sample <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated from the full training set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond static weighting, our key contribution lies in the introduction of adaptive class-aware modulation terms, <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are collectively referred to as global modulation coefficients. These terms respectively control inter-class repulsion and intra-class alignment strength. To compute these class-aware modulation coefficients, we begin by constructing a global class-to-class similarity matrix <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we distinguish between instance indices <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math>, which refer to samples within a batch, and class labels <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i},y_{j}\in\{1,\dots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i},y_{j}\in\{1,\dots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represent the corresponding class identities. Each element <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the average pairwise cosine similarity between all training embeddings of class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{y_{i},y_{j}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}|\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}|}\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}}\sum_{\xi^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}}\frac{\mathbf{z}_{\xi}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}}{\|\mathbf{z}_{\xi}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}\|_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{y_{i},y_{j}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}|\cdot|\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}|}\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}}\sum_{\xi^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}}\frac{\mathbf{z}_{\xi}^{\top}\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}}{\|\mathbf{z}_{\xi}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the index sets of all training samples belonging to class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. That is, <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represent sample indices such that <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>ξ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{\xi^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are their corresponding embeddings.
This similarity matrix serves as a globally informed semantic map, allowing the model to reason over full-dataset statistics rather than relying on potentially noisy or sparse batch-level interactions. Such global perspective is particularly beneficial in few-shot settings, where batch samples may not adequately reflect class-level distributions.
Next, to derive the intra-class alignment weight <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we focus on the diagonal elements <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{y_{i},y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{y_{i},y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which reflect the internal cohesiveness of each class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Classes with low self-similarity (i.e., high internal variance) require stronger alignment forces. Thus, we take the inverse of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{y_{i},y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{y_{i},y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply a temperature-scaled softmax normalization to obtain the intra-class modulation weights:</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}=\frac{\exp\left(\frac{1}{S_{y_{i},y_{i}}+\epsilon}/\tau_{\psi}\right)}{\sum\limits_{m=1}^{C}\exp\left(\frac{1}{S_{m,m}+\epsilon}/\tau_{\psi}\right)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}=\frac{\exp\left(\frac{1}{S_{y_{i},y_{i}}+\epsilon}/\tau_{\psi}\right)}{\sum\limits_{m=1}^{C}\exp\left(\frac{1}{S_{m,m}+\epsilon}/\tau_{\psi}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant for stability, and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the temperature. This formulation is both class-aware and semantically grounded, as it dynamically adjusts each class’s alignment strength based on its empirical compactness, while also allowing prior knowledge injection if needed.
In contrast, for inter-class repulsion weighting <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we emphasize relative separation among distinct classes. For each anchor class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute softmax attention over the off-diagonal elements of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{y_{i},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{y_{i},n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}=\begin{cases}\frac{\exp\left(S_{y_{i},y_{j}}/\tau_{\phi}\right)}{\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}n=1\\
n\neq y_{i}\end{subarray}}\limits^{C}\exp\left(S_{y_{i},n}/\tau_{\phi}\right)}&amp;\text{if }y_{i}\neq y_{j}\\
0&amp;\text{if }y_{i}=y_{j}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="1.400em">n</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">=</mo><mn mathsize="1.400em">1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="1.400em">n</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">≠</mo><msub><mi mathsize="1.400em">y</mi><mi mathsize="1.400em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi>C</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}=\begin{cases}\frac{\exp\left(S_{y_{i},y_{j}}/\tau_{\phi}\right)}{\sum\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}n=1\\
n\neq y_{i}\end{subarray}}\limits^{C}\exp\left(S_{y_{i},n}/\tau_{\phi}\right)}&amp;\text{if }y_{i}\neq y_{j}\\
0&amp;\text{if }y_{i}=y_{j}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature coefficient. This design allows the model to selectively emphasize harder negatives (i.e., classes more similar to the anchor), enabling a more nuanced and structurally interpretable inter-class contrastive signal. By framing contrastive repulsion in terms of soft attention over class-level similarity, we ensure that the model allocates training focus according to the semantic difficulty of each class pair, rather than treating all inter-class interactions uniformly as in conventional pairwise schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To further incorporate domain-specific semantic priors, we extend the formulation of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> by integrating user-defined prior knowledge. Specifically, we define a class-to-class semantic prior matrix <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> and an intra-class semantic prior vector <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\psi}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\psi}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each element encodes the strength of prior similarity or cohesion desired between specific classes. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the average values of the computed <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> across classes. We then refine the original coefficients as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\phi}_{y_{i},y_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\phi}_{y_{i},y_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}+\mathbf{P}_{\phi}[y_{i},y_{j}]\cdot\bar{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\phi_{y_{i},y_{j}}+\mathbf{P}_{\phi}[y_{i},y_{j}]\cdot\bar{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\psi}_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\psi}_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\psi_{y_{i}}+\mathbf{p}_{\psi}[y_{i}]\cdot\bar{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\psi_{y_{i}}+\mathbf{p}_{\psi}[y_{i}]\cdot\bar{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">By default, the prior matrices <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized to zero, making this augmentation optional. When specified, they allow explicit control over the relative strength of class interactions based on expert knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, to stabilize training and prevent oscillations due to frequent updates, we adopt a momentum-based update scheme for both <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. At each epoch <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficients are updated as exponential moving averages of their current and previous values:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda\cdot\phi^{(t-1)}+(1-\lambda)\cdot\tilde{\phi}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda\cdot\phi^{(t-1)}+(1-\lambda)\cdot\tilde{\phi}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda\cdot\psi^{(t-1)}+(1-\lambda)\cdot\tilde{\psi}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda\cdot\psi^{(t-1)}+(1-\lambda)\cdot\tilde{\psi}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\psi}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\psi}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the raw coefficients computed at epoch <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (including semantic prior adjustment), and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the momentum factor controlling the influence of historical estimates. At the first epoch (<math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized as the directly computed <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\psi}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\psi}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our formulation provides an adaptive, semantically grounded, and class-aware framework for regulating contrastive dynamics across intra- and inter-class relations. By incorporating momentum-based updates and optional prior knowledge, the coefficients <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> evolve smoothly with the embedding space, ensuring training stability and compatibility with stochastic optimization and random initialization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training Strategies</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, the training objective is composed of Focal Loss and the proposed Adaptive Global Class-Aware Contrastive Loss (AGCACL), which are combined with equal weight. Focal Loss with a focusing parameter <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> is employed to downweight easy samples and emphasize hard ones, while AGCACL leverages class prototypes and a global similarity matrix to enhance intra-class compactness and inter-class separability. During training, a class-balanced sampler is adopted to ensure uniform class distribution in each batch. The optimizer is Adam with a learning rate and weight decay of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size of 48, and a total of 30 epochs, without learning rate scheduling, gradient clipping, or mixed precision. For AGCACL, the temperature parameters are set as <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\phi}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\phi}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\psi}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\psi}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\alpha}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\alpha}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, with a momentum coefficient of 0.9. Class prototypes are initialized randomly in a 512-dimensional space and jointly optimized with network parameters. Class-frequency priors are derived from the empirical distribution <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[4987,\,1876,\,889,\,37,\,21,\,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>4987</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1876</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 889</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 37</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[4987,\,1876,\,889,\,37,\,21,\,6]</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, a semantic prior matrix is defined, where only the entries at positions <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(6,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(6,3)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,6)</annotation></semantics></math> are set to 1 while all others remain 0, thereby imposing stronger repulsion between these two easily confused classes. This training strategy enables more stable optimization under few-shot and class imbalance settings, improving both robustness and clinical relevance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted on the Leipzig Heart Center pediatric arrhythmia dataset. Data augmentation was applied only to the training set, while validation and test sets remained unchanged. For baselines, we reproduced the original architectures with minor adaptations (e.g., six-class output, consistent use of lead II, removal of irrelevant branches), and all models were trained from scratch without external pretraining. To ensure fairness, Focal Loss with <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> was uniformly adopted, and hyperparameters were aligned or lightly tuned on the validation set. For the comparison of contrastive losses, we additionally modified Focal InfoNCE into a supervised version by treating samples from the same class as positives. Experiments were conducted using two AMD EPYC 7B12 CPUs (64 cores, 128 threads, 2.25 GHz base frequency) and eight NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs (24 GB memory per GPU, 16,384 CUDA cores). Model performance was evaluated by Top-1 Accuracy, Macro Specificity, Precision, Recall, F1, and F2, where macro-averaging ensures equal weight to minority classes under class imbalance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results and Discussion</span></h2>

