<th id="S3.T5.2.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T5.2.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S3.T5.2.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">97.76</td>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">99.28</td>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">94.08</td>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">91.97</td>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{92.97}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">92.97</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{92.97}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T5.2.13.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{92.36}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">92.36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{92.36}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our proposed method achieves the best performance across six overall and macro-averaged metrics (Top-1 Accuracy, Macro Specificity, Macro Precision, Macro Recall, Macro F1, and Macro F2), with values of 97.76%, 99.28%, 94.08%, 91.97%, 92.97%, and 92.36%, respectively. Compared with the strongest prior value for each metric across all baselines (see Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>), our method achieves absolute and relative gains of +13.64 points Macro P (+16.96%), +15.96 points Macro R (+21.00%), +19.82 points Macro F1 (+27.10%),
and +19.44 points Macro F2 (+26.66%), while also maintaining consistent improvements in Accuracy and Specificity. Fig. <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> visualizes the four macro metrics with the largest improvements, highlighting that our method enhances sensitivity to minority classes without sacrificing overall specificity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The differences among baselines further reflect how model families adapt to pediatric ECG. The Transformer architecture adopted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> performs the worst, mainly because it relies heavily on large-scale training data and long-sequence modeling, which cannot be effectively leveraged under the single-lead, small-sample, shorter-sequence characteristics of pediatric ECG. In contrast, the CNN+LSTM architectures adopted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> perform relatively better: convolutional layers efficiently capture local waveform patterns, while LSTMs model temporal dependencies across beats, making them stronger than pure CNNs on several metrics. However, these methods still fail to adequately handle the long-tailed distribution, leading to poor coverage of minority classes and deficiencies on macro Recall and F-scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed method achieves consistent advantages thanks to the complementary roles of its components. The dual-branch ResNet encoders separately extract local and temporal features from surface ECG and IEGM, providing richer representations at the feature level. The cross-modal semantic attention fusion module aligns the two modalities at key semantic points, allowing them to complement and correct each other, thereby maintaining robustness under noise and complex morphologies. The lightweight Transformer classification head strengthens global dependency modeling on the fused features, improving generalization for minority and complex rhythms. Finally, the Adaptive Global Class-Aware Contrastive Loss (AGCACL) leverages class prototypes and
global similarity matrices to dynamically enforce intra-class compactness and enlarge inter-class margins, effectively mitigating long-tailed and few-shot problems. Together, these modules explain why our method not only outperforms existing approaches in overall accuracy, but also achieves substantial improvements on clinically critical minority classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond numerical gains, these improvements also have clinical significance.
Enhanced minority-class detection directly translates into a reduced risk of missed diagnoses, which is especially important for early identification of pediatric arrhythmias. The incorporation of IEGM provides complementary physiological evidence for complex rhythm interpretation, increasing the interpretability and confidence of predictions. The overall framework can also be seamlessly integrated into existing workflows from rhythm screening to electrophysiology evaluation, supporting clinical triage and pre-operative risk assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparative Analysis of Contrastive Losses</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we compared several representative contrastive loss functions under identical model and training settings. Overall results demonstrate that <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AGCACL</span> achieves the highest scores on <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Macro Recall, Macro F1, and Macro F2</span> (91.97 / 92.97 / 92.36), clearly outperforming other approaches, while maintaining competitive performance on <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-1 Accuracy and Macro Specificity</span>. Compared with Focal InfoNCE, AGCACL shows its strongest advantage on macro-averaged metrics sensitive to minority classes, indicating better robustness and clinical relevance under class imbalance and few-shot conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The differences with existing methods further confirm this observation. SupCon ignores class imbalance and fails to guarantee sufficient separability of minority classes in the embedding space. RCL incorporates class-frequency weighting but insufficiently handles hard negatives. Focal InfoNCE improves class separation by emphasizing hard negatives, but it still suffers from batch-level randomness and lacks global consistency. In contrast, AGCACL unifies <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">class prototypes, global similarity matrices, and adaptive weighting mechanisms</span> within one framework: it prioritizes separation of the most confusing negative pairs while strengthening intra-class compactness for minority categories with weaker internal consistency. This design enables more stable and targeted optimization of class boundaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamic evolution of inter-class similarity (<a href="#S3.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) highlights the unique behavior of AGCACL. At early training stages, all methods show similar separation across most class pairs. However, as training progresses, AGCACL continuously lowers similarity curves for easily confused pairs (e.g., class 6 vs. class 3, class 2 vs. class 3), whereas SupCon and RCL even exhibit rebounds in these positions, approaching or exceeding the baseline without contrastive loss. This indicates that AGCACL’s global weighting mechanism effectively directs optimization toward the “hardest negatives,” enabling faster and more stable boundary expansion. Meanwhile, AGCACL also decreases overall similarity at a faster and smoother rate than Focal InfoNCE. By leveraging global similarity matrices, AGCACL provides steadier scheduling, leading to smoother curve evolution and the lowest convergence levels on the test set. In comparison, intra-class similarity changes are simpler: AGCACL aggregates slightly more slowly in early training, but once prototypes stabilize, it quickly rises to levels comparable to or higher than other methods. Thus, its true advantage lies not in aggregation speed, but in sustained improvements of inter-class separability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Other visualizations further support this conclusion. Fig. <a href="#S3.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows inter-class similarity heatmaps on the test set. For the most confusing pairs (e.g., class 6 vs. class 3), AGCACL produces the lowest similarity, reflecting its “hard-negative prioritization” effect. At the same time, its overall heat level is the lowest, indicating robustness not dependent on batch sampling but balanced boundary expansion at the global scale. Fig. <a href="#S3.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents boxplots of intra-class compactness, calculated by cosine similarity between each sample and its class centroid. Results show that AGCACL improves compactness for most classes (cls1–cls4) compared with the setting without contrastive loss, while a few tail classes (cls5–cls6) are slightly weaker. Nevertheless, macro-level metrics improve substantially, confirming that under class imbalance, boundary optimization contributes more to recall gains than intra-class tightening alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, AGCACL’s key advantage lies in moving contrastive learning from “sample–sample pairs” to explicit “class–class relationships.” By leveraging class prototypes and global similarity matrices, and dynamically adjusting optimization direction with adaptive weights (<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>), AGCACL achieves more stable training and consistently enlarges class margins where they matter most. As a result, the model not only maintains strong overall accuracy, but also substantially improves recall and F-scores for clinically critical minority classes. For pediatric arrhythmia screening, this translates to a reduced risk of missed diagnoses, while the global similarity analysis and prototypes provide interpretability by clearly showing which categories are effectively separated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablation Studies</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a systematic ablation study to evaluate the contributions of four factors: the modality (Single vs. Dual, i.e., ECG only vs. ECG+IEGM), the fusion strategy (concatenation <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cat</em> vs. semantic attention <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attn</em>), the classification head (MLP vs. Transformer, denoted as TF), and the contrastive loss (None vs. AGCACL). The overall results are summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>, with additional visual comparisons provided in Fig. <a href="#S3.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A clear trend can be observed: introducing IEGM in the Dual setting significantly improves performance compared with the Single setting. Even without changing other modules, Dual increases Macro Precision from 74.58 to 91.49 and Macro Recall from 72.53 to 87.80, with F1 and F2 also improving by more than 15 percentage points. The scatter distribution in Fig. <a href="#S3.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> further illustrates this effect, as Dual-related points are clustered toward the upper-right quadrant, indicating simultaneous gains in both precision and recall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of fusion method also matters. Although direct concatenation yields competitive results when paired with an MLP, semantic attention fusion consistently performs better in the Dual setting, especially when combined with the Transformer head. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>, attention-based fusion achieves higher Macro Recall (89.87 vs. 87.80) and demonstrates clear advantages in the bar plots of Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>(b–d). Specifically, in the Macro Recall panel (Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>b), attn produces noticeably taller bars compared with cat, and in the Macro F1 and Macro F2 panels (Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>c–d), its superiority becomes even more pronounced.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding classification heads, the Transformer does not show clear advantages over MLP under concatenation, but in the presence of attention-based fusion, its global modeling capacity brings substantial improvements. As a result, the Dual+attn+TF configuration reaches the highest Top-1 Accuracy (98.12)
and Macro Specificity (99.40) in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>. This is also reflected in Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, where bars for this combination remain consistently higher across multiple metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The contrastive loss further strengthens these gains. Even in the Single setting, adding AGCACL already improves F1 by more than five points. In the Dual setting, the effect is amplified: Dual+cat+TF+AGCACL achieves the highest Macro Recall of 92.02 (Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>b), while Dual+attn+MLP+AGCACL yields the highest Macro Precision of 97.38 (Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>a). Most importantly, the Dual+attn+TF+AGCACL combination delivers the overall best results, achieving Macro F1 of 92.97 and Macro F2 of 92.36, as seen in Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>c–d, where its bars clearly surpass those of all other settings. The scatter plot in Fig. <a href="#S3.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> also highlights this effect: AGCACL consistently shifts configurations upward along the recall axis, and in the attn+TF setting it simultaneously preserves high precision, leading to point clusters concentrated in the upper-right region.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, the ablation confirms a consistent causal chain:
the Dual modality provides complementary physiological information;
attention fusion resolves cross-modal semantic gaps;
the Transformer head captures global dependencies;
and AGCACL, by prioritizing globally hard negatives,
further enlarges decision boundaries under class imbalance.
In synergy, these four components enable the model to substantially boost
Macro Recall, F1, and F2 without sacrificing overall Top-1 Accuracy or Macro Specificity. This balanced improvement is consistently demonstrated across
the numbers in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>,
the bar plots in Fig. <a href="#S3.F10" title="Fig. 10 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>,
and the precision–recall distribution in Fig. <a href="#S3.F9" title="Fig. 9 ‣ III-A Comparison with State-of-the-Art (SOTA) ‣ III Results and Discussion ‣ Advancing Few-Shot Pediatric Arrhythmia Classification with a Novel Contrastive Loss and Multimodal Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Limitations</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the promising results, this study has several limitations. First, the current work is constrained by the limited availability of pediatric arrhythmia datasets. In particular, some rhythm categories are concentrated in a few individuals, making it difficult to perform cross-subject generalization and raising concerns about model robustness when deployed in real-world clinical settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we proposed a multimodal end-to-end framework for pediatric arrhythmia classification, combining ECG and IEGM with semantic attention fusion, a lightweight Transformer encoder, and the newly introduced Adaptive Global Class-Aware Contrastive Loss (AGCACL). To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study on the Leipzig Heart Center pediatric/congenital ECG+IEGM dataset, accompanied by a complete, reproducible preprocessing pipeline. On this dataset, our method achieved the overall best performance, including 97.76% Top-1 Accuracy and 92.97% Macro F1, significantly outperforming strong baselines. Clinically, the framework enables earlier and more reliable detection of rare but high-risk arrhythmias, supporting screening, pre-procedural evaluation, and postoperative follow-up in pediatric and congenital heart disease. Although the dataset size and single-center setting remain limitations, future work will focus on multi-center validation and integrating physiological priors for greater generalization and interpretability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgment</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors would like to thank the team who made the dataset publicly available, as well as the patients, their families, and the clinical staff of the affiliated hospital for their contributions and support.</p>
</div>
</section>
